WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.560
NANDU: In a moment, it’ll start to make a noise

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:09.940
The aioli

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:11.940
Here we call it “to break”

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.280
It’s the noise you are listening

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.520
This noise means that

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.360
We are doing a good job, the emulsion is perfect

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.000
I believe is gonna be ok

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.000
now

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.140
This noise

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.000
means we are doing great.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.000
We almost have it.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.200
And we have it.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.420
We have it.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:54.020
To know if it's very thick

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.520
There's a way

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.900
That is to turn the mortar

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.780
Here we have a good emulsion, see?

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:08.220
A perfect emulsion.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.320
And very tasty.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.380
DAVID WEITZ: We saw Nandu make
one of the most delicious, most

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.190
impressive kinds of emulsions, aioli.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.950
Aioli is like a garlic mayonnaise.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.990
It's very simple ingredients.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:29.635
It's garlic, salt, and olive oil.

00:01:29.635 --> 00:01:32.010
And what he does is he mixes
the salt in with the garlic,

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.730
and he crushes the garlic.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:37.110
The salt pulls the
water out of the garlic.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:41.850
And then the water from the
garlic acts as the surfactant

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:46.770
to stabilize the drops of
the olive oil in the water.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.970
And you saw Nandu slowly
mix them together.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:51.590
He crushed the garlic.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:52.970
He pulled the water out.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:57.680
And he slowly mixed the olive
oil in, slowly emulsified it.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.580
And ultimately, he
made a solid emulsion.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.460
The only way the
emulsion can be solid is

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.700
if he emulsifies sufficient
olive oil that you

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.639
get these highly compressed drops.

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:12.080
So they become elastic.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.640
They become solid.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:17.330
And if you watch Nandu make
it, sometimes what he'll do

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:21.380
is he'll say, the emulsion's about
to break, just as he's finishing it.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:25.940
And then takes a few drops of
water and adds it to the emulsion.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:30.620
The reason for that is that you're
so close to phase inversion.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:36.050
You have so much olive oil and so little
water that it can easily phase invert.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.630
And instead of having
drops of oil in the water--

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:43.370
you have a huge number of drops of oil
in a relatively small amount of water--

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:46.730
and you can end up with
drops of water in oil.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:50.270
Then you have a very small
amount of water as drops in oil.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:51.650
But you don't have an emulsion.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.520
And you don't have a solid.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.940
Now, a simpler way of making an
aioli is to add a surfactant.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.340
Garlic is not the best surfactant.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.640
That's why it's very difficult
to make a true aioli.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.670
To make it much more easily,
what Nandu did was he

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.830
added a really wonderful surfactant.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:11.240
So he took an egg yolk.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.640
He mixed it with garlic and the water.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:19.520
And that then could much more easily
emulsify all of the olive oil.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.130
Without the egg yolk,
it's called allioli,

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:25.910
and that's the most simplest
form of an emulsion.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:29.420
It's also, to me, one of the
most impressive forms of emulsion

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:32.450
because it's so little,
so few ingredients,

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:36.110
and yet you get this solid,
garlic-like mayonnaise.

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:39.270
Eventually, however,
emulsions will fail.

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:44.030
So let's understand a little bit about
what it means to have an emulsion fail.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.370
In order for an emulsion
to fail, you have

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:50.120
to lose the drops of
one fluid in the second.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:54.360
So you have to lose the drops
of the oil in the water.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.000
And there are several
ways that this can happen.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.830
The first thing that will happen is
the fact that the oil and the water

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:02.330
have a different density--

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:05.510
typically the oil is
lighter than the water--

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:09.420
eventually, the oil drops
will come to the surface.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:13.970
This will particularly be the case if
the volume fraction is relatively low.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:17.160
And there are several
ways that this can occur.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:20.839
The most obvious way
is through coalescence.

00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:23.750
Two drops come close to
one another, and they

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.900
join together and form a larger drop.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:31.340
Always for the failure of an
emulsion, you have to lose the drops.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:34.110
And so the drops have to become larger.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:38.420
It's the small drops that give the
emulsion its particular character.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:42.590
But there is another more pervasive
way that emulsions can fail

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:44.300
and that drops can grow.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.760
And that is called Ostwald ripening.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:48.900
And that is absolutely pervasive.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.970
It's almost impossible to completely
eliminate Ostwald ripening.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:56.100
Fortunately, it's a
relatively slow process,

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:59.000
so it's not so important
oftentimes, unless you

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.730
store an emulsion for a very long time.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:08.600
And Ostwald ripening is the transfer
of fluid from one drop to another drop.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:13.910
And remember, drops are at a higher
internal pressure than the outside.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:16.970
And the pressure inside--
the La Place pressure--

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:20.400
increases as the drops gets smaller.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.730
So there is a driving
force-- an increased

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:28.540
pressure-- to drive the fluid from
the small drops into the large drops.

